WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:01.439
People think they know the story.

00:00:01.440 --> 00:00:03.119
They think it's a story about one

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:04.649
man. But when you pull back the

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:05.939
curtain, it's actually about so much

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:06.940
more.

00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:10.879
A recent Netflix documentary series

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:12.709
called "Madoff: The Monster of

00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:14.449
Wall Street" by Emmy Award winning

00:00:14.450 --> 00:00:16.249
filmmaker Joe Berlinger tells the

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:18.139
story of the largest

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:20.419
Ponzi scheme in history.

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:22.279
How could he fool so many people

00:00:22.280 --> 00:00:23.719
for so long?

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:26.689
What happened was so avoidable

00:00:26.690 --> 00:00:28.609
and so many people enabled it to

00:00:28.610 --> 00:00:30.140
happen because of greed.

00:00:31.190 --> 00:00:32.449
The film's other villain is the

00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:34.759
Securities and Exchange Commission,

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:36.349
which received complaints about

00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:39.229
Madoff starting in the early 1990s,

00:00:39.230 --> 00:00:40.969
and yet not only failed to catch

00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:42.829
him, but helped enable his

00:00:42.830 --> 00:00:43.879
fraud.

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:45.829
During one investigation, all

00:00:45.830 --> 00:00:48.019
SEC investigators had to do

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:49.609
was check the routing number to

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:51.559
verify a trade had actually

00:00:51.560 --> 00:00:53.719
occurred—which it hadn't, of course,

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:55.549
because all of Madoff's trades

00:00:55.550 --> 00:00:56.239
were fake.

00:00:56.240 --> 00:00:57.679
You could have shut Madoff down in

00:00:57.680 --> 00:00:59.599
one half hour by just following

00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:01.429
up on one of his allegations that

00:01:01.430 --> 00:01:03.349
they were not conducting trades.

00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:05.268
If you are the watchdog, you have

00:01:05.269 --> 00:01:06.949
totally and thoroughly failed in

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:08.449
your mission.

00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:09.769
Don't you get it?

00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:11.689
The Netflix series acknowledges that

00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:13.519
the SEC is complicit in

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:15.529
Madoff's scam and that he could

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:17.689
have been caught if one investigator

00:01:17.690 --> 00:01:19.099
assigned to the case had done about

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:20.989
30 minutes of checking.

00:01:20.990 --> 00:01:22.789
They asked the question, they got

00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:23.869
the information, they got the

00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:25.759
account number, but they never made

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:26.760
the phone call.

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:31.009
Inconceivable, and I never

00:01:31.010 --> 00:01:33.169
got an answer

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:35.539
that made any sense, to be honest,

00:01:35.540 --> 00:01:37.369
from the people that we

00:01:37.370 --> 00:01:39.199
questioned under oath in

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:41.029
depositions about

00:01:41.030 --> 00:01:42.230
this particular point.

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:45.400
They forgot, they missed it,

00:01:46.690 --> 00:01:49.569
but it just doesn't make any sense.

00:01:49.570 --> 00:01:51.549
It also blames deregulation and

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:53.079
free market capitalism for making

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:54.819
the fraud possible.

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:56.469
The first episode sets the stage

00:01:56.470 --> 00:01:58.329
with a speech by Ronald Reagan.

00:01:58.330 --> 00:02:00.639
Government that is high taxes,

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:01.959
excessive spending and

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:03.849
overregulation, had

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:05.499
thrown a wrench in the works of our

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:06.909
free markets.

00:02:06.910 --> 00:02:08.739
Our economic program for the next

00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:10.478
four years, we're going to turn the

00:02:10.479 --> 00:02:11.479
ball loose.

00:02:13.540 --> 00:02:15.609
In episode three, journalist Diana

00:02:15.610 --> 00:02:17.229
Henriques makes the connection

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:18.189
explicit.

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:20.199
Resources that had been

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:22.089
in New York City on Wall

00:02:22.090 --> 00:02:24.099
Street's doorstep devoted

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:26.499
to white collar crime.

00:02:26.500 --> 00:02:28.179
It's a fraud.

00:02:28.180 --> 00:02:30.279
Were being steered away.

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:32.379
Were being funneled into

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:34.269
counterterrorism and

00:02:34.270 --> 00:02:36.129
terrorism finance issues.

00:02:38.100 --> 00:02:39.629
For the SEC, this was an

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:42.539
exacerbation of an existing problem

00:02:42.540 --> 00:02:45.149
as a result of a deregulatory

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:46.589
campaign that began with the

00:02:46.590 --> 00:02:49.439
election of Ronald Reagan in 1980.

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:51.929
In this present crisis,

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:53.939
government is not the solution to

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:55.499
our problem.

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:57.210
Government is the problem.

00:02:59.850 --> 00:03:02.099
The laws that they could rely

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:04.139
on to pursue investigations

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:05.999
had been tightened and their budgets

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:08.339
had been cut and cut again.

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:09.779
And yet nothing in the film would

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:11.549
lead one to draw the conclusion that

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:13.439
the SEC needed a bigger budget

00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:14.759
to catch Madoff.

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:16.589
In fact, outsiders were

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:18.599
sounding the alarm without access

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:20.429
to government funding or regulatory

00:03:20.430 --> 00:03:21.269
muscle.

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:23.519
In 2001, Barron's journalist

00:03:23.520 --> 00:03:25.439
Erin Arvedlund reported that

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:27.149
many Wall Street investors were

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:29.069
suspicious that Madoff was engaged

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:31.409
in foul play, and the SEC received

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:33.029
its first complaint that Madoff was

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:34.919
running an unregistered investment

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:37.079
company, offering 100%

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:40.469
safe investments, in 1992.

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:42.719
In 1999, a derivatives expert

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:44.759
named Harry Markopolos, who worked

00:03:44.760 --> 00:03:46.379
at a competing firm, started to

00:03:46.380 --> 00:03:48.149
alert the SEC that Madoff's

00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:50.279
investment returns were virtually

00:03:50.280 --> 00:03:51.929
impossible.

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:53.789
In 2005, Markopolos

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:56.429
sent the agency an infamous 25-page

00:03:56.430 --> 00:03:58.379
memo explaining why the world's

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:00.749
largest hedge fund is a fraud.

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:02.609
The SEC opened an investigation in

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:04.349
2006 and then closed it the

00:04:04.350 --> 00:04:06.449
following year because the

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:09.089
uncovered violations were remedied

00:04:09.090 --> 00:04:11.039
and those violations were not so

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:13.409
serious as to warrant an enforcement

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:14.159
action.

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:16.259
We knew then that we had provided

00:04:16.260 --> 00:04:18.239
enough red flags and

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:20.069
mathematical proofs

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:21.239
where they should have been able to

00:04:21.240 --> 00:04:23.069
shut him down right then and

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:24.070
there.

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:26.369
Unfortunately, as they didn't

00:04:26.370 --> 00:04:28.079
respond to my written submissions in

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:30.179
2000, 2001,

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:32.759
2005, 2007

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:34.679
and 2008, here we

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:35.579
are today.

00:04:35.580 --> 00:04:37.529
So how is this tale of

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:39.269
epic failure on the part of a

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:41.069
government agency the fault of

00:04:41.070 --> 00:04:42.689
deregulation?

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:44.579
Instead of making lazy allusions

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:45.929
to the evils of free market

00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:47.909
capitalism, to better understand the

00:04:47.910 --> 00:04:49.979
lessons of the Madoff saga, director

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:51.479
Joe Berlinger should have consulted

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:52.829
the work of the free market

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:54.719
economist George Stigler,

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:56.699
who won the Nobel Prize in part

00:04:56.700 --> 00:04:58.559
for his work on regulatory

00:04:58.560 --> 00:04:59.759
capture.

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:01.829
In a 1971 paper, "The Theory

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:03.659
of Economic Regulation," Stigler

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:05.369
argues that while many people

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:06.719
believe that regulation is

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:08.459
instituted primarily for the

00:05:08.460 --> 00:05:09.869
protection and benefit of the

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:11.729
public, in fact, it

00:05:11.730 --> 00:05:13.589
mainly serves the purposes of the

00:05:13.590 --> 00:05:16.079
largest companies being regulated,

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:18.209
which form a symbiotic

00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:20.069
relationship with their regulatory

00:05:20.070 --> 00:05:21.070
overseers.

00:05:46.830 --> 00:05:48.539
The essay focuses mainly on how

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:50.549
regulators help existing companies

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:53.249
by protecting them from competition,

00:05:53.250 --> 00:05:55.289
but Stigler's theory can

00:05:55.290 --> 00:05:57.209
also be used to better understand

00:05:57.210 --> 00:05:59.159
the SEC's failure to catch

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:00.299
Madoff.

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:02.549
In a 1972 essay, Stigler

00:06:02.550 --> 00:06:05.009
writes that the regulating agency

00:06:05.010 --> 00:06:07.379
must eventually become the agency

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:09.689
of the regulated industry.

00:06:09.690 --> 00:06:11.609
They need each other.

00:06:11.610 --> 00:06:13.469
That's because the career lawyers at

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:15.569
the SEC making the decisions

00:06:15.570 --> 00:06:17.579
have much more to gain personally

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:19.349
from having a positive relationship

00:06:19.350 --> 00:06:21.089
with big industry players than

00:06:21.090 --> 00:06:22.230
antagonizing them.

00:06:23.340 --> 00:06:24.719
This is also what happened in the

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:26.819
case of Sam Bankman-Fried,

00:06:26.820 --> 00:06:28.649
who was close with politicians

00:06:28.650 --> 00:06:30.689
and regulators and actively lobbied

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:32.129
for regulation of the crypto

00:06:32.130 --> 00:06:34.079
industry in a bid to

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:36.269
elbow out competitors—right

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:37.919
up to the moment that his crypto

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:39.899
exchange FTX collapsed

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:41.609
under the weight of its own alleged

00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:42.689
fraud.

00:06:42.690 --> 00:06:44.819
This cozy relationship between

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:46.289
industry and regulators helps

00:06:46.290 --> 00:06:48.539
explain why the SEC

00:06:48.540 --> 00:06:50.429
continually missed what was

00:06:50.430 --> 00:06:52.199
right in front of them in the Madoff

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:52.799
case.

00:06:52.800 --> 00:06:54.659
When Harry Markopolos was

00:06:54.660 --> 00:06:56.849
trying to persuade the

00:06:56.850 --> 00:06:59.129
SEC that

00:06:59.130 --> 00:07:01.559
a gigantic, historically

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:03.569
huge Ponzi scheme was

00:07:03.570 --> 00:07:05.879
being run at that very minute

00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:07.199
by Bernie Madoff,

00:07:08.610 --> 00:07:10.649
he was facing an enormous

00:07:10.650 --> 00:07:12.659
challenge because the SCC

00:07:12.660 --> 00:07:14.579
relied on Bernie Madoff

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:16.709
as a source of Wall Street wisdom.

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:21.059
He had a tremendous reputation

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:22.949
as being one of the real giants

00:07:22.950 --> 00:07:25.079
of Wall Street, not only

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:27.119
for developments that

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:29.429
he helped launch, changes

00:07:29.430 --> 00:07:31.439
in the industry that he helped

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:33.389
define, but

00:07:33.390 --> 00:07:35.249
he also spent

00:07:35.250 --> 00:07:37.169
a lot of time working with

00:07:37.170 --> 00:07:39.209
the SEC and other government

00:07:39.210 --> 00:07:41.519
agencies on panels and

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:43.349
assisting with

00:07:43.350 --> 00:07:45.599
how to regulate business.

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:47.519
Madoff even spread the rumor

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:49.349
that he was being considered as

00:07:49.350 --> 00:07:50.933
a future chairman of the SEC.

00:07:52.050 --> 00:07:54.119
At one point, he tells them that

00:07:54.120 --> 00:07:56.219
he is on the short list to become

00:07:56.220 --> 00:07:57.570
the next head of the SEC.

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:01.169
And there's e-mail communication

00:08:01.170 --> 00:08:03.299
between these two junior examiners

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:05.309
and their boss, where the boss says

00:08:05.310 --> 00:08:07.109
to them, in joking, maybe you can be

00:08:07.110 --> 00:08:08.639
Bernie's aides when he comes to take

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:09.640
over the SEC.

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:13.049
So they're awestruck to begin

00:08:13.050 --> 00:08:13.529
with.

00:08:13.530 --> 00:08:15.059
Can all of this depend on Ronald

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:16.829
Reagan, deregulation and the free

00:08:16.830 --> 00:08:17.249
market?

00:08:17.250 --> 00:08:19.139
One guy led you to

00:08:19.140 --> 00:08:20.879
this pile of dung that is, that is

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:22.739
Bernie Madoff and stuck your nose in

00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:24.689
it and you couldn't figure it out.

00:08:24.690 --> 00:08:26.849
Is the SEC too cozy

00:08:26.850 --> 00:08:27.809
with the industry?

00:08:27.810 --> 00:08:29.639
Are they captive by this industry or

00:08:29.640 --> 00:08:31.109
are they looking out for us, looking

00:08:31.110 --> 00:08:32.788
out for the taxpayers?

00:08:32.789 --> 00:08:34.259
The documentary also makes a strong

00:08:34.260 --> 00:08:36.359
case that the SEC didn't just

00:08:36.360 --> 00:08:38.158
fail to catch Madoff.

00:08:38.159 --> 00:08:40.048
Investors were emboldened

00:08:40.049 --> 00:08:41.759
by the agency's investigation of

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:43.678
Madoff and failure to find serious

00:08:43.679 --> 00:08:45.539
violations such as this

00:08:45.540 --> 00:08:47.339
man who gave Madoff the proceeds

00:08:47.340 --> 00:08:49.319
from the sale of his small business.

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:51.179
The fact that the SEC found no

00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:53.159
evidence of wrongdoing was,

00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:55.169
to me, putting a stamp of approval

00:08:55.170 --> 00:08:56.369
on Madoff.

00:08:56.370 --> 00:08:58.289
So I sent

00:08:58.290 --> 00:09:00.389
a wire for $900,000

00:09:00.390 --> 00:09:01.390
to Madoff.

00:09:04.899 --> 00:09:06.909
"That regulation is primarily

00:09:06.910 --> 00:09:08.319
for the protection and benefit of

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:09.909
the public at large or some large

00:09:09.910 --> 00:09:11.529
subclass of the public," Stigler

00:09:11.530 --> 00:09:13.899
writes, is a commonly held view.

00:09:13.900 --> 00:09:14.900
But it's not.

00:09:15.820 --> 00:09:17.589
SEC investigators didn't want to be

00:09:17.590 --> 00:09:19.599
overly combative with Madoff because

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:21.429
they thought doing so could hurt

00:09:21.430 --> 00:09:22.959
their careers.

00:09:22.960 --> 00:09:24.909
Regulation exists largely

00:09:24.910 --> 00:09:27.069
to serve the interests of the people

00:09:27.070 --> 00:09:28.989
being regulated.

00:09:28.990 --> 00:09:30.849
The illusion of competent regulation

00:09:30.850 --> 00:09:32.439
gave investors a false sense of

00:09:32.440 --> 00:09:33.999
security that drove them to make

00:09:34.000 --> 00:09:35.739
colossal mistakes.

00:09:35.740 --> 00:09:37.329
Can the answer really be more

00:09:37.330 --> 00:09:38.259
regulation?

00:09:38.260 --> 00:09:40.659
What if Harry Markopolos's warnings

00:09:40.660 --> 00:09:41.799
hadn't been filtered through the

00:09:41.800 --> 00:09:42.800
SEC?

00:09:42.970 --> 00:09:44.139
The average person is better

00:09:44.140 --> 00:09:45.879
equipped to spot a con man than we

00:09:45.880 --> 00:09:47.139
give him credit for.

00:09:47.140 --> 00:09:48.609
But the myth that regulators are

00:09:48.610 --> 00:09:50.829
primarily motivated to protect

00:09:50.830 --> 00:09:53.109
the interests of the public causes

00:09:53.110 --> 00:09:55.539
us to suspend our better judgment.

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:30.339
The big takeaway from "Madoff: The

00:10:30.340 --> 00:10:32.079
Monster of Wall Street" is that

00:10:32.080 --> 00:10:34.479
regulators unwittingly facilitated

00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:36.339
his fraud just as they

00:10:36.340 --> 00:10:38.229
helped Sam Bankman-Fried run his

00:10:38.230 --> 00:10:39.789
alleged con.

00:10:39.790 --> 00:10:42.339
As you watch the Netflix series,

00:10:42.340 --> 00:10:44.319
think about how much more time

00:10:44.320 --> 00:10:46.149
or power should we really

00:10:46.150 --> 00:10:47.469
give them?

00:10:47.470 --> 00:10:49.539
Are they helping us

00:10:49.540 --> 00:10:50.620
or themselves?

